## ASP.NET Framework → ASP.NET Core 8 Migration

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Response.End()` / `Response.Redirect(..., true)`** — These relied on `Thread.Abort`, which **does not exist** in .NET Core. There is no equivalent.
2. **Web Forms event model** (`btnExport_Click`) — Gone entirely. Use MVC actions or Minimal APIs.
3. **`Response.Clear()` / `Response.AddHeader()`** — Replaced by returning typed results.
4. **`ThreadAbortException` catch blocks** — Dead code in Core; remove them.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Authorization;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc;

public class ReportController : Controller
{
    // POST /Report/Export
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Export()
    {
        var data = GenerateReport();

        // Return a FileResult — the framework handles headers and content type.
        // No Response.Clear/Write/End needed.
        return File(
            System.Text.Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes(data),
            "text/csv",
            "report.csv"
        );
    }

    // POST /Report/Submit
    [HttpPost]
    public IActionResult Submit()
    {
        // In Core, use [Authorize] or check manually — but never call
        // Response.Redirect with endResponse.
        if (!User.Identity?.IsAuthenticated ?? true)
        {
            return Redirect("/login");
            // Redirect() returns a RedirectResult; execution continues
            // past this point only if you DON'T return. Always return.
        }

        try
        {
            ProcessRequest();
            return Redirect("/success");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            LogError(ex);
            return Redirect("/error");
        }
        // No ThreadAbortException catch — it cannot happen in Core.
    }

    private string GenerateReport() => /* ... */ "";
    private void ProcessRequest() { /* ... */ }
    private void LogError(Exception ex) { /* ... */ }
}
```

### Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Writing to `Response.Body` after `Redirect()`** | In Framework, `endResponse: true` aborted the thread so later code never ran. In Core, execution **continues** after `Redirect()`. You **must `return`** the `IActionResult`. |
| **Swallowing `ThreadAbortException`** | This pattern masks real bugs in Core — remove it entirely. |
| **`Response.Write()` in actions** | Don't write directly to the response stream when returning `IActionResult`. The two conflict. Use `File()`, `Content()`, or `Ok()`. |
| **`Response.End()` replacements** | There is none. Structure your action to return a single result and exit. If you have middleware writing to the body, call `return` or use `HttpContext.Abort()` (kills the connection — not the same thing). |
| **Auth checks** | Prefer the `[Authorize]` attribute over manual `IsAuthenticated` checks: |

```csharp
[Authorize]          // returns 401/redirects to login automatically
[HttpPost]
public IActionResult Submit()
{
    // User is guaranteed authenticated here
    try
    {
        ProcessRequest();
        return Redirect("/success");
    }
    catch (Exception ex)
    {
        LogError(ex);
        return Redirect("/error");
    }
}
```

### Required `Program.cs` Setup

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
builder.Services.AddControllersWithViews();
builder.Services.AddAuthentication(/* scheme config */);
builder.Services.AddAuthorization();

var app = builder.Build();
app.UseAuthentication();
app.UseAuthorization();
app.MapControllerRoute("default", "{controller=Home}/{action=Index}/{id?}");
app.Run();
```

**TL;DR** — The core mental model shift: Framework actions controlled the response stream imperatively and used `Thread.Abort` to bail out. Core actions **return result objects** and rely on normal control flow (`return`). Remove all `Response.End/Clear/Write/Redirect(..., true)` and `ThreadAbortException` patterns.
